Assign the visible score of this particular item, not the reputation of its author or a model-quality benchmark score.
Consider the event's consequence for the AI field, the concrete new facts actually conveyed, and how broad the practical or societal impact is. AI relevance alone does not earn a high score. A short but explicit announcement of a genuinely consequential event can deserve a high score; length, excitement, first-party status, or a famous name alone cannot.
Use this editorial scale:
- 0–19: reactions, greetings, vague praise, unsupported speculation, image/showcase captions with little stated news.
- 20–34: minor observations, promotion, niche demonstrations or reminders with limited concrete novelty.
- 35–49: useful but routine updates, product integrations, narrow research or practical information with limited reach.
- 50–64: substantive new tools/models, meaningful research, detailed evidence or industry developments.
- 65–79: major releases, large strategic investments, consequential policy/legal developments or broad, well-supported impacts.
- 80–100: exceptional field-changing breakthroughs or events with unusually large, clearly stated consequences.
The ranges are guides, not quotas. Judge the actual report, including substantive quoted content present in it. Do not infer details from an unseen link or fetch anything. Publication metadata describes the news period; do not penalize every item merely because you are evaluating an archive. Explain the concrete event/information and why it belongs near the chosen score.

